; A reference journal for `hledger-x fmt --explicit`.
;
; Every case --explicit changes, and the notable ones it deliberately leaves
; alone. The file is valid hledger, so the two can be compared directly:
;
;   hledger-x fmt --explicit --diff tests/golden/explicit.in.ledger
;   hledger print -x -f tests/golden/explicit.in.ledger
;
; Accounts are namespaced per transaction so every balance assertion stands on
; its own and any case can be reordered or deleted on its own.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Declared commodity styles. Only a *declared* style enables decimal padding;
; a commodity with no directive is never padded.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

commodity 1,000.00 EUR
commodity $1,000.00
commodity 1_000.00 SEK
commodity CHF
    format 1,000.000 CHF

; Every declaration is positional. hledger displays amounts journal-wide in
; the style it ends up with, but it *parses* a file top to bottom, so an
; amount written above its commodity's directive is read without it — and
; restyling such an amount can change what it means. GBP is declared by the
; `D` further down, and everything in GBP sits below it except the one case
; immediately above it, which shows what happens when it is not yet known.

commodity 1 000.00 USD

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Filling in the amount hledger would infer.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

2026-01-01 one posting left blank
    ; the classic case: the remainder is worked out and written in
    t01:expenses          1,234.00 EUR
    t01:assets           -1,234.00 EUR

2026-01-02 inferred through a unit cost
    ; the cost side is what balances, so the blank posting gets EUR
    t02:broker              10.000 CHF @ 1.10 EUR
    t02:assets              -11.00 EUR

2026-01-03 inferred through a total cost
    t03:broker              10.000 CHF @@ 11.00 EUR
    t03:assets              -11.00 EUR

2026-01-04 a posting carrying only a balance assertion
    ; the amount goes in front of the assertion, which is left alone
    t04:expenses             10.00 EUR
    t04:assets              -10.00 EUR = -10.00 EUR

2026-01-05 all four assertion operators
    ; =, ==, =* and ==* all leave the posting's contribution alone
    t05:a                    10.00 EUR = 10.00 EUR
    t05:b                    10.00 EUR == 10.00 EUR
    t05:c                    10.00 EUR =* 10.00 EUR
    t05:d                    10.00 EUR ==* 10.00 EUR
    t05:equity              -40.00 EUR

2026-01-06 a cost with its amount attached
    ; `@1.1EUR` is one token, not three
    t06:broker              10.000 CHF @ 1.10 EUR
    t06:assets              -11.00 EUR

2026-01-07 an assertion with its amount attached
    t07:expenses             10.00 EUR
    t07:assets              -10.00 EUR = -10.00 EUR

2026-01-08 everything already balances
    ; the remainder is zero, and an explicit zero is still written
    t08:expenses             10.00 EUR
    t08:assets              -10.00 EUR
    t08:rounding              0.00 EUR

2026-01-09 a remainder spanning two commodities
    ; the blank posting becomes one posting per commodity
    t09:broker               10.00 EUR
    t09:broker               10.00 SEK
    t09:assets              -10.00 EUR
    t09:assets              -10.00 SEK

2026-01-10 the three posting kinds balance separately
    ; (...) contributes to nothing; [...] balances among its own kind
    t10:expenses             10.00 EUR
    (t10:budget)             10.00 EUR
    t10:assets              -10.00 EUR
    [t10:tracked]             4.00 EUR
    [t10:mirror]             -4.00 EUR

2026-01-12 a status flag does not change which group a posting is in
    ; `* (v)` is still an unbalanced virtual posting, and `! [w]` still
    ; balances among its own kind — the flag is not part of the account
    t12:expenses             10.00 EUR
    * (t12:budget)            3.00 EUR
    t12:assets              -10.00 EUR
    ! [t12:tracked]           4.00 EUR
    ! [t12:mirror]           -4.00 EUR

2026-01-11 unitless amounts stay unitless
    ; nothing here names a commodity and no `D` is in effect yet — the one
    ; below is positional — so the filled-in amount has none either
    t11:steps                10000
    t11:balance             -10000

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Padding to the commodity's declared decimal places. `fmt` on its own never
; does this — it is the second half of what --explicit means.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

2026-02-01 a declared right-side style
    t21:expenses          1,234.00 EUR
    t21:assets           -1,234.00 EUR

2026-02-02 a declared attached-left style
    t22:expenses         $1,234.00
    t22:assets          $-1,234.00

2026-02-03 the sign written before the symbol
    ; `-$1234` and `$-1234` are one commodity, and both render hledger's way
    t23:expenses        $-1,234.00
    t23:assets           $1,234.00

2026-02-04 an underscore as the digit group mark
    t24:expenses          1_234.00 SEK
    t24:assets           -1_234.00 SEK

2026-02-05 a style declared by a `format` subdirective
    t25:expenses         1,234.000 CHF
    t25:assets          -1,234.000 CHF

2026-02-07 costs and assertions are padded too
    ; both sides of the `@`, and the assertion's own amount
    t27:broker              10.000 CHF @ 1.10 EUR
    t27:assets              -11.00 EUR = -11.00 EUR

2026-02-09 a space as the digit group mark
    ; a space between digit groups is part of the number, not the boundary
    ; with the commodity — `1 234.00 USD` is one amount
    t29:expenses      1 234 567.00 USD
    t29:assets       -1 234 567.00 USD

2026-02-10 a space-grouped amount already in the journal
    ; and it lines up with an ungrouped one in the same column
    t30:expenses          1 234.00 USD
    t30:assets              -50.00 USD
    t30:rest             -1 184.00 USD

2026-02-08 more precision than declared is kept
    ; the declared places are a floor, never a ceiling: rounding would lose
    ; value, so 4.0001 is left exactly as written
    t28:expenses            4.0001 EUR
    t28:assets             -4.0001 EUR

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Commodities with no directive. Nothing is padded — but an amount that has to
; be *generated* still has to be written somehow, and it copies the posting it
; balances against.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

2026-03-01 undeclared, symbol on the right with a space
    t31:crypto                 0.5 BTC
    t31:equity                -0.5 BTC

2026-03-02 undeclared, symbol attached on the left
    t32:expenses              Kc10
    t32:assets               Kc-10

2026-03-03 undeclared, symbol attached on the right
    t33:expenses             10sat
    t33:assets              -10sat

2026-03-04 undeclared, symbol first
    t34:expenses            NOK 10
    t34:assets             NOK -10

2026-03-05 typed digit grouping is carried into the generated amount
    t35:expenses          1,234.50 JPY
    t35:assets           -1,234.50 JPY

2026-03-06 the style may come from a cost tail rather than a face
    t36:broker                  10 DKK @ Kc1.1
    t36:assets             Kc-11.0

2026-03-07 a unitless amount keeps the grouping it was written with
    ; no commodity to declare a style, so the sibling's own notation is
    ; copied — answering `1 000` with a bare `-1000` read as badly as
    ; answering `$10` with `-10 $`
    t37:steps                1 000
    t37:balance             -1 000

2026-04-03 a style is never applied above its own declaration
    ; GBP's style says `,` groups digits — but this is written above the `D`
    ; that says so, where hledger has not read it yet and `1,234 GBP` would
    ; mean 1.234. Grouping `1234` here would multiply it by a thousand, so
    ; the amount is left exactly as written and nothing is padded
    t43:expenses              1234 GBP
    t43:assets               -1234 GBP

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The `D` directive. It does three things, all of them from this line on: it
; names the commodity of amounts written without one, it declares a display
; style for that commodity, and — alone among the directives — it says which
; character is the decimal mark when reading an amount in *any* commodity that
; declares no style of its own. --explicit writes the first of those out;
; spelling out what the journal already says is the whole point of the flag.
; The unitless amounts above keep no commodity, being above this line.
;
; `add` never does this. Its default commodity comes from the configuration
; alone, so a bare amount somebody types cannot quietly pick one up from the
; journal.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

D 1,000.00 GBP

2026-05-03 a style declared by `D`
    ; `D` declares a display style for its own commodity, like `commodity`
    t26:expenses          1,234.00 GBP
    t26:assets           -1,234.00 GBP

2026-05-04 `D` also says which mark is decimal, for every commodity
    ; unlike `commodity`, whose style is its own commodity's business, a `D`
    ; sample decides how an amount in a commodity that declares no style
    ; reads: under `1,000.00` the `,` groups digits, so this is fifteen
    ; hundred zloty rather than one and a half
    t63:expenses             1,500 PLN
    t63:assets              -1,500 PLN

2026-05-01 a bare amount takes the default commodity
    t61:expenses             10.00 GBP
    t61:assets              -10.00 GBP

2026-05-02 an explicit commodity still wins over the default
    t62:expenses             10.00 GBP
    t62:expenses              5.00 EUR
    t62:assets              -10.00 GBP
    t62:assets               -5.00 EUR

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Deliberately left alone.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

2026-04-01 an undeclared commodity is never padded
    ; `1 PLN` stays `1 PLN`; only a declared style says what "filled out" means
    t41:expenses                 1 PLN
    t41:assets                  -1 PLN

2026-04-02 lot notation is not understood
    ; hledger would fill `$-50` here from the lot cost. hledger-x cannot read
    ; `{...}`, so the amount does not parse and the whole transaction is left
    ; as it stands rather than guessed at
    t42:shares                  10 AAPL {$5}
    t42:cash

2026-04-04 a lone tab means different things to different hledgers
    ; every released hledger reads the first account here as `t44:a<tab>1 EUR`
    ; holding no amount; an unreleased build on hledger's master branch reads
    ; it as `t44:a` holding `1 EUR`. The two disagree about which posting is
    ; waiting for an amount, so neither gets one written in
    t44:a	1 EUR
    t44:b                    -1.00 EUR

~ monthly  rent
    ; a periodic rule's postings are laid out like any others, but a rule is
    ; not a transaction: nothing in it is balanced, filled in or restyled
    t51:expenses              1234 EUR
    t51:assets

= t52:expenses
    ; an auto-posting rule is not a transaction either. Its `*2` is a
    ; multiplier rather than an amount, and is only right-aligned
    (t52:budget)                *2

comment
A comment block is prose, not journal syntax. Nothing in here is filled in,
padded or aligned, even though it looks exactly like a transaction:

2026-09-09 not a transaction
    a  1234 EUR
    b
end comment
